## MINORS

- **AC-20 deferral is DEFENSIBLE — no exploitable hole NOW.** I tried to break this: can a parity verdict artifact be mutated post-attest and still pass F2? Per design doc `:150-151`, AC-21 already provides atomic-rollback so `attest_parity` mid-mutation leaves file-SHA == DB-SHA; AC-20 is the *separate* `bth check`-driven drift-detection feature. The feature is unshipped (no parity runs exist), so deferring verdict-artifact SHA reconciliation to T8 creates no live attack surface. Removing it from W3 coverage is honest. **Concur with the note.**
- **C-a (cool minimal / SLURM-safe) is preserved.** Adding one nullable TEXT column to the atomic-write Parquet adds no extra files between cool-write and compact. Verified against `to_arrow`/`write_run` path. I could not break this.

---

## What I tried to break and could not
- The column-promotion premise itself (R-A vs R-B/R-C) — R-B genuinely cannot satisfy C-b without a warm-repopulation path; R-A is the right call.
- `migrate_catalog` schema-driven backfill — confirmed it needs no per-version edit (modulo the `_default_array` null-tuple addition in M-2).
- AC-20 exploitability — no live hole; deferral sound.

**Net:** R-A is the right design; the note's change set will silently NULL the new column in at least three places (compact INSERT, the nested write-path read, and the cool-fallback) if implemented as written. Fix B-1/B-2/B-3 and fold in M-1/M-2 and this is implementable.

